---
import Layout from "../../layouts/Layout.astro";
import { Image } from "astro:assets";
import argusLibrary from "../../assets/work/argus-library.png";

// Case-study copy is transcribed from the BRO-2025 content-draft document
// (revision 9, approved by Eric 2026-08-11). Two blocks in that draft are
// production notes rather than reader-facing copy and are deliberately not
// rendered here: the pricing/metering "out of scope" note, and the line
// recording that Eric supplied the screenshot — the screenshot itself is the
// evidence, so it appears as a figure instead.
//
// Every Evidence row below was re-verified against the live product on
// 2026-08-11 before shipping: argus.build renders "50k+ assets analyzed"
// and "< 3s avg" on its homepage, and argus.build/docs documents the
// pagination envelope and the MCP tool names quoted in the body.

const description =
    "Argus is a media asset catalog that reads an image the moment it lands — palette, mood, composition — and exposes the same asset graph over both a REST API and a hosted MCP server. A case study by Eric Brookfield.";
---

<Layout title="Argus — Case study — Eric Brookfield" description={description}>
    <main class="case">
        <div class="container">
            <header class="case-header">
                <a href="/work" class="back-link">Back to work</a>
                <p class="eyebrow">Case study</p>
                <h1>ARGUS</h1>
                <p class="case-meta">
                    <span>Solo build</span>
                    <span class="meta-sep">/</span>
                    <span>2026</span>
                    <span class="meta-sep">/</span>
                    <a href="https://argus.build" target="_blank" rel="noopener">argus.build</a>
                </p>
                <p class="lede">
                    A media asset catalog that reads an image the moment it
                    lands and writes down what's actually in it, so the search
                    that follows is about meaning rather than filenames.
                </p>
            </header>

            <!-- ===== 1. The problem ===== -->
            <section class="block" aria-labelledby="problem">
                <h2 class="section-head" id="problem">The problem</h2>
                <div class="prose">
                    <p>
                        Every creative team ends up with the same problem, just
                        at a different scale: a library of thousands of photos
                        and graphics, sitting in folders named by date or by
                        whoever exported them last, and no way to find "the
                        warm, celebratory hero shot" inside that pile without
                        opening every file by hand. Manual tagging is the fix
                        everyone reaches for, and it's also the fix that never
                        finishes — it's backlog work, and backlog work always
                        loses to whatever shipped yesterday. What I wanted was
                        something that reads an image the moment it lands and
                        writes down what's actually in it — palette, mood,
                        composition, visual language — so the search that
                        follows is about meaning, not filenames.
                    </p>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ===== 2. Constraints ===== -->
            <section class="block" aria-labelledby="constraints">
                <h2 class="section-head" id="constraints">Constraints</h2>
                <div class="prose">
                    <p>
                        I built Argus alone, nights and weekends, around
                        everything else — no team, no outside budget, nothing
                        resembling a deadline. It started as an itch, not a
                        roadmap item, and I think that shows in the decisions
                        below: there was no one else in the room to weigh in,
                        so each one got made once, and had to be right enough
                        to live with.
                    </p>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ===== 3. What I decided and why ===== -->
            <section class="block" aria-labelledby="decisions">
                <h2 class="section-head" id="decisions">What I decided and why</h2>
                <div class="prose">
                    <p>
                        There's one decision here worth explaining, because
                        from the outside it looks like foresight, and it really
                        wasn't.
                    </p>
                    <h3 class="decision">
                        Two API surfaces from day one — REST and MCP — instead
                        of just one.
                    </h3>
                    <p>
                        Argus exposes the same asset graph two ways: a REST API
                        for anything that isn't an agent, and an MCP server at
                        <code>argus.build/mcp</code> for Claude and other agents
                        to call directly. I didn't build both because I'd mapped
                        out some future where agents mattered more than scripts
                        — I built both because I had two different use cases in
                        mind at the same time, and testing them in parallel
                        turned out to cost almost nothing, since they share the
                        same test suite underneath. I still don't know which use
                        case wins. Building both just meant I didn't have to
                        decide yet.
                    </p>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ===== 4. How it was built ===== -->
            <section class="block" aria-labelledby="architecture">
                <h2 class="section-head" id="architecture">How it was built</h2>
                <div class="prose">
                    <p>
                        Here's how it actually works, mechanically. Upload
                        triggers analysis asynchronously — the API hands back a
                        response in under 3 seconds and the actual vision pass
                        runs in the background, so the write path never blocks
                        on a model call. Analysis results (palette, mood, tags)
                        land in a searchable index; semantic queries like "warm,
                        celebratory hero shot" run against that index and come
                        back ranked by relevance rather than exact match.
                        Authentication runs through two token types: long-lived
                        <code>ak_</code>-prefixed API keys for normal
                        integrations, and short-lived <code>at_</code>-prefixed
                        agent session tokens that can't mint new keys or
                        escalate privileges — a narrower blast radius for
                        anything an agent is holding mid-transcript.
                    </p>
                    <h3 class="decision">The MCP server.</h3>
                    <p>
                        This is the piece other engineers will actually want
                        detail on. It's a hosted endpoint at
                        <code>argus.build/mcp</code>, not a local process — an
                        agent connects to it the way it'd connect to any other
                        MCP server, no separate SDK required. It exposes over 20
                        tools — <code>search_assets</code>,
                        <code>get_asset</code>, <code>upload_from_url</code>,
                        <code>generate_style_guide</code>, and
                        <code>adapt_svg_to_palette</code> among them — the same
                        operations available over REST, just wrapped for
                        tool-calling instead of HTTP verbs. That parity is the
                        whole point of building both surfaces: an agent working
                        through MCP and a script hitting REST are working
                        against the identical asset graph, not two versions of
                        the truth.
                    </p>
                </div>

                <!-- ===== Architecture diagram =====
                     Two hand-authored inline SVGs — wide for desktop, tall for
                     narrow screens — swapped with the same 768px media query
                     the rest of the site flips at. No JS, no runtime Mermaid;
                     the viewBox fixes the aspect ratio so neither one shifts
                     layout while it loads. Accent is spent only on the MCP
                     branch, because that branch is the argument of the page. -->
                <figure class="diagram">
                    <svg
                        class="diagram-wide"
                        viewBox="0 0 900 440"
                        aria-hidden="true"
                        focusable="false"
                    >
                        <defs>
                            <marker
                                id="argus-arrow-muted"
                                viewBox="0 0 10 10"
                                refX="9"
                                refY="5"
                                markerWidth="7"
                                markerHeight="7"
                                orient="auto-start-reverse"
                            >
                                <path d="M0,0 L10,5 L0,10 z" class="head-muted" />
                            </marker>
                            <marker
                                id="argus-arrow-accent"
                                viewBox="0 0 10 10"
                                refX="9"
                                refY="5"
                                markerWidth="7"
                                markerHeight="7"
                                orient="auto-start-reverse"
                            >
                                <path d="M0,0 L10,5 L0,10 z" class="head-accent" />
                            </marker>
                        </defs>

                        <!-- ingest chain -->
                        <path class="edge" d="M250,29 H286" marker-end="url(#argus-arrow-muted)" />
                        <path class="edge" d="M570,29 H606" marker-end="url(#argus-arrow-muted)" />
                        <path class="edge" d="M755,58 V110 H450 V146" marker-end="url(#argus-arrow-muted)" />

                        <!-- fork: shared stem, then one branch per surface -->
                        <path class="edge" d="M450,208 V240" />
                        <path class="edge" d="M450,240 H190 V266" marker-end="url(#argus-arrow-muted)" />
                        <path class="edge-accent" d="M450,240 H710 V266" marker-end="url(#argus-arrow-accent)" />
                        <path class="edge" d="M190,328 V376" marker-end="url(#argus-arrow-muted)" />
                        <path class="edge-accent" d="M710,328 V376" marker-end="url(#argus-arrow-accent)" />

                        <!-- nodes -->
                        <rect class="node" x="0" y="0" width="250" height="58" />
                        <text class="node-label" x="125" y="35">Image uploaded</text>

                        <rect class="node" x="290" y="0" width="280" height="58" />
                        <text class="node-label" x="430" y="26">Claude vision analysis</text>
                        <text class="node-sub" x="430" y="45">async, under 3s</text>

                        <rect class="node" x="610" y="0" width="290" height="58" />
                        <text class="node-label" x="755" y="26">Palette, mood, tags</text>
                        <text class="node-sub" x="755" y="45">written to the index</text>

                        <rect class="node" x="325" y="150" width="250" height="58" />
                        <text class="node-label" x="450" y="185">Semantic search index</text>

                        <rect class="node" x="65" y="270" width="250" height="58" />
                        <text class="node-label" x="190" y="305">REST API</text>

                        <rect class="node node-accent" x="585" y="270" width="250" height="58" />
                        <text class="node-label" x="710" y="296">MCP server</text>
                        <text class="node-sub" x="710" y="315">argus.build/mcp</text>

                        <rect class="node" x="65" y="380" width="250" height="58" />
                        <text class="node-label" x="190" y="415">Web app, scripts</text>

                        <rect class="node" x="585" y="380" width="250" height="58" />
                        <text class="node-label" x="710" y="415">Claude, other agents</text>
                    </svg>

                    <svg
                        class="diagram-tall"
                        viewBox="0 0 340 580"
                        aria-hidden="true"
                        focusable="false"
                    >
                        <defs>
                            <marker
                                id="argus-arrow-v-muted"
                                viewBox="0 0 10 10"
                                refX="9"
                                refY="5"
                                markerWidth="7"
                                markerHeight="7"
                                orient="auto-start-reverse"
                            >
                                <path d="M0,0 L10,5 L0,10 z" class="head-muted" />
                            </marker>
                            <marker
                                id="argus-arrow-v-accent"
                                viewBox="0 0 10 10"
                                refX="9"
                                refY="5"
                                markerWidth="7"
                                markerHeight="7"
                                orient="auto-start-reverse"
                            >
                                <path d="M0,0 L10,5 L0,10 z" class="head-accent" />
                            </marker>
                        </defs>

                        <path class="edge" d="M170,58 V94" marker-end="url(#argus-arrow-v-muted)" />
                        <path class="edge" d="M170,156 V192" marker-end="url(#argus-arrow-v-muted)" />
                        <path class="edge" d="M170,254 V290" marker-end="url(#argus-arrow-v-muted)" />

                        <path class="edge" d="M170,352 V386" />
                        <path class="edge" d="M170,386 H92 V412" marker-end="url(#argus-arrow-v-muted)" />
                        <path class="edge-accent" d="M170,386 H247 V412" marker-end="url(#argus-arrow-v-accent)" />
                        <path class="edge" d="M92,474 V510" marker-end="url(#argus-arrow-v-muted)" />
                        <path class="edge-accent" d="M247,474 V510" marker-end="url(#argus-arrow-v-accent)" />

                        <rect class="node" x="20" y="0" width="300" height="58" />
                        <text class="node-label" x="170" y="35">Image uploaded</text>

                        <rect class="node" x="20" y="98" width="300" height="58" />
                        <text class="node-label" x="170" y="124">Claude vision analysis</text>
                        <text class="node-sub" x="170" y="143">async, under 3s</text>

                        <rect class="node" x="20" y="196" width="300" height="58" />
                        <text class="node-label" x="170" y="222">Palette, mood, tags</text>
                        <text class="node-sub" x="170" y="241">written to the index</text>

                        <rect class="node" x="20" y="294" width="300" height="58" />
                        <text class="node-label" x="170" y="329">Semantic search index</text>

                        <rect class="node" x="20" y="416" width="145" height="58" />
                        <text class="node-label node-label-narrow" x="92" y="450">REST API</text>

                        <rect class="node node-accent" x="175" y="416" width="145" height="58" />
                        <text class="node-label node-label-narrow" x="247" y="442">MCP server</text>
                        <text class="node-sub node-sub-narrow" x="247" y="459">argus.build/mcp</text>

                        <rect class="node" x="20" y="514" width="145" height="58" />
                        <text class="node-label node-label-narrow" x="92" y="540">Web app,</text>
                        <text class="node-label node-label-narrow" x="92" y="558">scripts</text>

                        <rect class="node" x="175" y="514" width="145" height="58" />
                        <text class="node-label node-label-narrow" x="247" y="540">Claude,</text>
                        <text class="node-label node-label-narrow" x="247" y="558">other agents</text>
                    </svg>

                    <figcaption>
                        An upload triggers a Claude vision pass in the
                        background; palette, mood and tags land in the semantic
                        search index. Both surfaces read the same index — the
                        REST API for scripts and the web app, the MCP server for
                        agents. The two branches are the same asset graph, not
                        two versions of the truth.
                    </figcaption>
                </figure>
            </section>

            <!-- ===== 5. Evidence ===== -->
            <section class="block" aria-labelledby="evidence">
                <h2 class="section-head" id="evidence">Evidence</h2>
                <p class="block-intro">
                    Every claim above points at something you can go and check.
                </p>

                <figure class="shot">
                    <Image
                        src={argusLibrary}
                        alt="The Argus library view at argus.build: a workspace switcher set to Brookfield Digital, a semantic search field, a left rail of color, view, sort, project, status and tag filters, and a grid of 347 analyzed assets sorted newest first."
                        widths={[600, 900, 1800]}
                        sizes="(max-width: 768px) 100vw, 860px"
                        loading="lazy"
                    />
                    <figcaption>
                        The real library view, not a mockup: the Brookfield
                        Digital workspace with 347 analyzed assets, the semantic
                        search field, and the color and tag filters those
                        analyses populate.
                    </figcaption>
                </figure>

                <div class="entry">
                    <p class="entry-source">Live</p>
                    <div class="entry-body">
                        <p>
                            <a href="https://argus.build" target="_blank" rel="noopener">argus.build</a>
                            — the search, upload, and analysis flow described
                            above is running today.
                        </p>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="entry">
                    <p class="entry-source">Scale &amp; speed</p>
                    <div class="entry-body">
                        <p>
                            50,000+ assets analyzed, under 3 seconds average
                            analysis time — both figures are the product's own
                            counters, published on the
                            <a href="https://argus.build" target="_blank" rel="noopener">argus.build</a>
                            homepage.
                        </p>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="entry">
                    <p class="entry-source">Schema</p>
                    <div class="entry-body">
                        <p>
                            Search and list endpoints return one pagination
                            envelope, documented publicly rather than described:
                        </p>
                        <pre class="artifact"><code>GET /assets &rarr; &#123; total, limit, offset, hasMore, assets &#125;</code></pre>
                        <p>
                            The same shape backs the MCP
                            <code>search_assets</code> tool. Full reference:
                            <a href="https://argus.build/docs" target="_blank" rel="noopener">argus.build/docs</a>.
                        </p>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="entry">
                    <p class="entry-source">BRO-1090</p>
                    <div class="entry-body">
                        <h3>An agent rebuilt a real deck through the MCP server</h3>
                        <p>
                            The acceptance gate for Argus's own Design Skills
                            library was this: rebuild a real
                            <a href="https://brkdllc.com" target="_blank" rel="noopener">brkdllc.com</a>
                            deck end to end through the Argus MCP server, with
                            zero hand-carried context.
                            <code>get_brand_kit</code> pulled the real palette
                            (<code>#FFFFFF</code>, <code>#111111</code>,
                            <code>#DC2626</code>) and typography tokens.
                            <code>search_assets</code> semantically matched five
                            real cataloged images — hero, product demo,
                            pricing, problem statement, for-builders — with no
                            broken slots. The result was a 10-slide HTML deck
                            with keyboard navigation, touch swipe, and print
                            export, assembled by an agent rather than a human
                            picking assets.
                        </p>
                        <p>
                            That's the part I couldn't have predicted from the
                            spec: Argus isn't only cataloging images, it's a
                            brand kit an agent can build real deliverables from
                            unassisted.
                        </p>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ===== 6. What I'd do differently ===== -->
            <section class="block" aria-labelledby="differently">
                <h2 class="section-head" id="differently">What I'd do differently</h2>
                <div class="prose">
                    <p>
                        If I'm honest, there's nothing I'd change. Argus was
                        never about shipping fast — it was about sitting with
                        every piece of the problem long enough to actually
                        understand it: what creative teams need from a tool like
                        this, and just as importantly, what kills the experience
                        when an agent gets it wrong.
                    </p>
                </div>
            </section>

            <aside class="next">
                <a href="/work" class="next-link">More case studies</a>
            </aside>

            <footer class="site-footer">
                <div class="footer-grid">
                    <a href="/">Home</a>
                    <a href="mailto:eric@ericbrookfield.com">eric@ericbrookfield.com</a>
                    <a href="https://brkdllc.com" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Brookfield Digital</a>
                    <a href="https://github.com/ericdfields" target="_blank" rel="noopener">GitHub (@ericdfields)</a>
                    <a href="https://linkedin.com/in/ericbrookfield" target="_blank" rel="noopener">LinkedIn</a>
                </div>
            </footer>
        </div>
    </main>
</Layout>

<style>
    .case {
        padding: var(--spacing-xl) 0 0;
    }

    .case .container {
        max-width: 900px;
    }

    /* ====== HEADER ====== */
    .back-link {
        display: inline-block;
        font-family: var(--font-mono);
        font-size: var(--text-mono);
        color: var(--color-text-muted);
        text-transform: uppercase;
        letter-spacing: 0.08em;
        margin-bottom: 2rem;
    }

    .back-link::before {
        content: "← ";
        color: var(--color-accent);
    }

    .back-link:hover {
        color: var(--color-accent);
    }

    .eyebrow {
        font-family: var(--font-mono);
        font-size: var(--text-mono);
        color: var(--color-accent);
        text-transform: uppercase;
        letter-spacing: 0.12em;
        margin-bottom: 0.75rem;
    }

    .case-header h1 {
        font-size: clamp(2.6rem, 9vw, 5.5rem);
        line-height: 0.9;
        letter-spacing: -0.04em;
        text-transform: uppercase;
        margin-bottom: 1.25rem;
    }

    .case-meta {
        display: flex;
        flex-wrap: wrap;
        gap: 0.6rem;
        font-family: var(--font-mono);
        font-size: var(--text-mono);
        text-transform: uppercase;
        letter-spacing: 0.08em;
        color: var(--color-text-muted);
        margin-bottom: 1.75rem;
    }

    .case-meta a {
        color: var(--color-text);
        border-bottom: 1px solid var(--color-rule-heavy);
    }

    .case-meta a:hover {
        color: var(--color-accent);
        border-color: var(--color-accent);
    }

    .meta-sep {
        color: var(--color-rule-heavy);
    }

    .lede {
        max-width: 62ch;
        font-size: clamp(1rem, 1.35vw, 1.2rem);
        line-height: 1.55;
        color: var(--color-text);
    }

    /* ====== BLOCKS ====== */
    .block {
        padding-top: var(--spacing-lg);
        padding-bottom: var(--spacing-md);
    }

    .section-head {
        font-size: var(--text-h2);
        font-weight: 700;
        letter-spacing: 0.06em;
        text-transform: uppercase;
        margin-bottom: 1.25rem;
    }

    .block-intro {
        max-width: 58ch;
        margin-bottom: var(--spacing-md);
        color: var(--color-text-muted);
    }

    .prose {
        max-width: 66ch;
    }

    .prose p + p {
        margin-top: 1.1rem;
    }

    .decision {
        margin-top: 1.75rem;
        margin-bottom: 0.75rem;
        font-size: 1.0625rem;
        font-weight: 600;
        letter-spacing: -0.01em;
        line-height: 1.45;
    }

    /* Inline code carries long unbreakable identifiers (`adapt_svg_to_palette`
       is 21 characters). `anywhere` only breaks them when the line genuinely
       can't fit, which is the difference between an ugly wrap on a 320px phone
       and a horizontal scrollbar on the document. */
    code {
        font-family: var(--font-mono);
        font-size: 0.9em;
        color: var(--color-text);
        overflow-wrap: anywhere;
    }

    /* ====== DIAGRAM ====== */
    .diagram {
        margin: var(--spacing-lg) 0 var(--spacing-md);
        padding: var(--spacing-md) 0;
        border-top: 1px solid var(--color-rule);
        border-bottom: 1px solid var(--color-rule);
    }

    /* Class-on-class so the wide/tall toggle outranks the bare `.diagram svg`
       sizing rule — the same specificity trap Phase 1 hit on /practice. */
    .diagram svg {
        width: 100%;
        height: auto;
    }

    .diagram .diagram-wide {
        display: block;
    }

    .diagram .diagram-tall {
        display: none;
    }

    .node {
        fill: none;
        stroke: var(--color-rule-heavy);
        stroke-width: 1;
    }

    .node-accent {
        stroke: var(--color-accent);
    }

    .node-label {
        fill: var(--color-text);
        font-family: var(--font-mono);
        font-size: 14px;
        text-anchor: middle;
    }

    .node-sub {
        fill: var(--color-text-muted);
        font-family: var(--font-mono);
        font-size: 11px;
        text-anchor: middle;
    }

    /* The tall diagram scales up more (narrower viewBox), so hold the labels at
       roughly the same optical size as the wide one. */
    .diagram-tall .node-label {
        font-size: 13px;
    }

    .diagram-tall .node-label-narrow {
        font-size: 11px;
    }

    .diagram-tall .node-sub {
        font-size: 10px;
    }

    .diagram-tall .node-sub-narrow {
        font-size: 9px;
    }

    .edge {
        fill: none;
        stroke: var(--color-rule-heavy);
        stroke-width: 1;
    }

    .edge-accent {
        fill: none;
        stroke: var(--color-accent);
        stroke-width: 1;
    }

    .head-muted {
        fill: var(--color-rule-heavy);
    }

    .head-accent {
        fill: var(--color-accent);
    }

    figcaption {
        margin-top: var(--spacing-md);
        max-width: 58ch;
        font-size: var(--text-small);
        line-height: 1.6;
        color: var(--color-text-muted);
    }

    /* ====== SCREENSHOT ======
       Astro's <Image> emits intrinsic width/height, so the box is reserved
       before the file lands and nothing reflows. */
    .shot {
        margin: 0 0 var(--spacing-lg);
    }

    .shot :global(img) {
        display: block;
        width: 100%;
        height: auto;
        border: 1px solid var(--color-rule);
    }

    /* ====== EVIDENCE ENTRIES ======
       Same source rail as /practice: the citation sits beside the claim. */
    .entry {
        display: grid;
        grid-template-columns: clamp(6rem, 12vw, 9rem) 1fr;
        gap: 2rem;
        padding: var(--spacing-md) 0;
        border-top: 1px solid var(--color-rule);
    }

    .entry:last-child {
        border-bottom: 1px solid var(--color-rule);
    }

    .entry-source {
        font-family: var(--font-mono);
        font-size: var(--text-mono);
        color: var(--color-accent);
        text-transform: uppercase;
        letter-spacing: 0.08em;
        line-height: 1.6;
